{
  "dom_id": "step-e5811",
  "name": "Example: Identifying the Graph of a Polynomial",
  "problem": "Which of the following could be the graph of $y =2x^3- 6 x^2 +8?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13928579/assets/3e01f41691c4f157964157f30678f98977ac8bdf9908d89bd3e9a8817a6e2b91.png)",
  "worked_solution": "Let's call $P(x)$ the given polynomial:\n\n$$\nP(x)=2x^3 -6 x^2 + 8\n$$\n\nThe degree ($n=3$) is odd and the leading coefficient ($a_3 = 2$) is positive. Therefore, the polynomial has the following end behavior:\n\n- $P(x)$ approaches $-\\infty$ as as $x$ approaches $-\\infty.$\n\n- $P(x)$ approaches $\\infty$ as $x$ approaches $\\infty.$\n\nOnly the graph $\\textbf{C}$ satisfies these conditions.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
